r/value "all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421248} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b9589-1097-5d3e-a736-dde5e2438be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1886b4c5-85d1-54e5-8510-86fe8c320098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421248} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28908a1-f4e3-564f-a061-6af102ecf123" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c75ba2f7-e873-5513-857a-f8ee74ebb547" :question/math-academy-id "q-334026" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${{e}^{x},\\,x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{k}_{2}\\cdot x=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${k}_{1}$ and ${k}_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=0,$ we get  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get  \n{{field-2}}.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}{e}^{x}+{k}_{2}x=0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(0)=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n- If $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=0\\,⇒\\,2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{independent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546a4ff5-ae08-5b2d-af1e-53c8fe140e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421263} {:answer/id #uuid "85790f7f-c60c-58be-9db4-3349cd6ff648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421274} {:answer/id #uuid "85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421275} {:answer/id #uuid "c850c71d-7d08-5a39-bf9b-f0c43f99ce6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421275} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e342e39-3482-5d77-a23d-de51cf8fa0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a517c0a-31b2-510c-8c55-64c0349a20c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421250} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421251} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f38bb7-a295-503f-99c8-be2e7ecf6bfb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600b1bd0-4a54-55ee-8f3d-ea639c33d64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421266} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421270} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a2b6ff-740b-5c73-94e2-69f51c63499e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b78d46d-4b29-5944-9afd-75dc247769fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334119" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=-3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3x & x+1 \\\\ (-3x)^{′} & (x+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3x & x+1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3x)(1)-(x+1)(-3) \\\\  & =-3x+3(x+1) \\\\  & =-3x+3x+3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a767ea-664e-50a2-a2a9-ec9361cfa274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305990} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8826e3-2ec4-5554-86d5-cc7e46b0422c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8826e3-2ec4-5554-86d5-cc7e46b0422c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306501} :answer-field/id #uuid "94aa5ac7-2d4a-5d7f-9c4e-6fb13a1afc3e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16996d4c-2b6c-5166-8105-980226657e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305353} {:answer/id #uuid "2315271b-6c07-5281-bbfe-463dcd686f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305485} {:answer/id #uuid "58a292a9-e84f-5706-b13f-f91b2426500f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306003} {:answer/id #uuid "f062f1aa-af14-5fca-9ecb-15f58bf17800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16996d4c-2b6c-5166-8105-980226657e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305353} :answer-field/id #uuid "a548607e-4ac1-535f-9c60-003214855934" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8b6286-efcc-531e-bc94-8986c43bc303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306475} {:answer/id #uuid "a00cb01f-5d04-5a1f-bc9d-38298a86d590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00cb01f-5d04-5a1f-bc9d-38298a86d590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306652} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf71c9df-84b5-5944-afd6-3194ac26fbd6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a747558-5780-50c9-a35b-84cd8e00bad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334120" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40772f11-35fc-5f4f-bda3-7d228823b749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421258} {:answer/id #uuid "4526afa8-bdbb-5355-a82f-899c0edd5093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421261} {:answer/id #uuid "8096790f-b2a3-5ac1-9707-fe01377b65c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421272} {:answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421273} {:answer/id #uuid "c81d1e22-0260-5ad3-9abd-8fe77f612e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421273} :answer-field/id #uuid "f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334252" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305324} {:answer/id #uuid "490c271b-9e2e-51db-a620-2f1605eefe48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305860} {:answer/id #uuid "86a02ffc-3022-5a2c-ba39-4a3aab938d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306435} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f3082-0fa6-527a-b75f-81251bbbee57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306493} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f96877-d442-5c94-acec-552c52c92c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13f2a1b2-0fff-5c3e-b982-e7d57c3b60a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305324} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c02124-a667-54aa-af69-f5fffebd4c39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334278" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec064a4d-7102-57fc-b186-402daf8b164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150169} {:answer/id #uuid "189c918e-7d05-5308-8e1d-edf7c3216627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150170} {:answer/id #uuid "4c66de07-d217-52ea-a332-34af866e7b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150171} {:answer/id #uuid "53649d44-816e-5797-bda4-28adc599b7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421262} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5866a6-934d-5826-8186-efe3dbb229ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec064a4d-7102-57fc-b186-402daf8b164b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150169} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8068aa-dbbd-5b69-9937-8b4236d86198" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd30eee5-3768-5759-bb64-72e553a332ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-64710" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{2x}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{e}^{-2x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-2{e}^{-2x})-{e}^{-2x}(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
